2186045417] [17592186084280 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e705e91-32b9-5486-b6d0-c6ebfbc45c9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084280 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084281 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084281 #edb/ref 1061 34200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084281 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "44919f35-fd65-50af-b639-7d5eb83ab3b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084281 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084381 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084381 #edb/ref 1061 73696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084381 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13ee6ddf-a2c8-5588-8e52-f8a84af5eba4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084381 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084382 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084382 #edb/ref 1061 73697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084382 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a2043004-3f75-55f7-8ceb-e7960310878d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084382 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084383 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084383 #edb/ref 1061 73700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084383 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a2714886-a7e1-52ee-ba1b-b1922a69c5ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084383 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084384 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084384 #edb/ref 1061 73701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084384 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85378e79-6c45-5c8a-9009-8d28559db885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084384 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084385 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084385 #edb/ref 1061 73702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084385 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "abd1e9c3-f22d-5627-bb7d-521c942987a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084385 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084386 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084386 #edb/ref 1061 73705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084386 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "381f23e4-4ced-5b30-8ea4-c24b78bb3ff1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084386 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084387 #edb/ref 1061 73708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084387 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cc41227-f39d-5f7a-a0e4-998e7c74302e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084387 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084484 #edb/ref 1064 "The **lowest common multiple** (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of two integers is the smallest multiple that's common to both numbers.\n\nTo find the lowest common multiple of two small numbers, we can list all multiples of the two numbers and identify the smallest multiple that they have in common.\n\nFor example, let's find the lowest common multiple of $4$ and $6.$ We start by listing all multiples of $4$ and $6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & \\,\\to \\,4,\\,8,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,\\,… \\\\ 6 & \\,\\to \\,6,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,18,\\,24,\\,30,\\,36,\\,…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe smallest multiple in both lists is ![](images/92/9285d37c7b455395acb42a54f78348bedfd8c9a72ad63d6e6ab7c2426148ea0d.svg) So $12$ is our answer, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)= 12.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The lowest common multiple is also sometimes called the *least* common multiple." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084484 #edb/ref 1065 5301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084484 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b16f2464-2c19-5166-bd61-97043695afcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084484 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084485 #edb/ref 1064 "Now suppose that we want to find the lowest common multiple of two larger numbers. It would take a while to make long lists of multiples. Luckily, there is a faster method. \n\nLet's demonstrate by finding $\\textrm{LCM}(10,12).$ We start by writing the prime factorization of $10$ and $12$, writing any repeating factors as powers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5={2}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\ 12 & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write down each unique prime factor that appears in either of our decompositions. In our case, the unique prime factors are \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}, \\qquad {\\color{red}3}, \\qquad {\\color{red}5}.\n$$\n\nNow, for each number in our list, we look at *both* prime factorizations and we take the *largest* power shown for each prime factor:\n\n- The factors corresponding to ${\\color{red}2}$ are ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$ Of these, the larger power is ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}3}.$ It is ${\\color{red}3}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}5}.$ It is ${\\color{red}5}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\nFinally, we multiply the largest powers of each factor together, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(10,12) & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084485 #edb/ref 1065 5489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084485 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "de44c642-c9d2-5c1d-8b59-8781a99559ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084485 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Larger Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084509 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $\\triangle{ABC}$ be a triangle where the lengths $AB=7$ and $AC=5$ are fixed. For example, consider the triangle shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c1df256d9a40c50e3535f3055fef041cf02710c0a9e0414acae1bf38681ff4.png)\n\n      \n\nNow, suppose that we vary the angle $\\angle{A}$, making it either very small or very large. What would be the maximum possible length of the side $\\overline {BC}$ for which the figure is still a triangle?  \n\nWe make the longest possible segment $\\overline{BC}$ by increasing the angle $\\angle{A}$ as shown in the sketches below. Here, we fix vertices $A$ and $B$, and then \"rotate\" the side $\\overline{AC}$ about the vertex $A$ until $\\angle{A}$ measures $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/d9/d9777d021ad9aac118452b691567d325939c5c8591b777f2cf9c17b39916e660.png)\n\n          \n\nFrom the picture, we can see that when $m\\angle{A}=180^\\circ ,$ we will not have a triangle anymore and the length of $\\overline{BC}$ will be $7+5=12.$ Therefore, if we would like to have a triangle, $\\overline{BC}$ must be shorter than the sum of the other sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & <AB+AC \\\\  & <7+5 \\\\  & <12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is true for all triangles:\n\nIn every triangle, the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nThe above statement is one part of the **triangle inequality theorem**, which we will continue building upon in this lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084509 #edb/ref 1065 8192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084509 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "29fc4755-2259-5126-bad7-5e7c73c355b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084509 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084510 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the same triangle as before, with sides $5$ and $7.$ This time, what is the shortest possible length of the side $\\overline {BC},$ for which the figure is still a triangle?\n\nWe make the shortest possible segment $\\overline{BC}$ by decreasing the angle $\\angle{A}.$ Again, we fix $A$ and $B$, and then \"rotate\" the side $\\overline{AC}$ about the vertex $A$ towards the side $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d1bd3363c2c218c6a2813a957bace3d29b7240919ced63d58be299e9478c099.png)\n\n         \n\nFrom the picture, we can see that for $m\\angle{A}=0^\\circ$ we will not have a triangle anymore and the length of $\\overline{BC}$ will be $7-5=2.$ Therefore, if we would like to have a triangle, $\\overline{BC}$ must be longer than the difference of the other sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & >AB-AC \\\\  & >7-5 \\\\  & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is true for all triangles: \n\nIn every triangle, the length of any one side must be larger than the difference of the other two sides.\n\nThe above statement is the other part of the **triangle inequality theorem**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084510 #edb/ref 1065 8193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084510 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "64f2a60c-c156-5226-a994-9c2f141a59db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084510 #edb/ref 1067 "The Difference of Two Sides" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084511 #edb/ref 1064 "All of the discussion so far can be summarized in the following statement that is called the **triangle inequality theorem**.\n\n*In a triangle, the length of a side must be greater than the difference of the lengths of the other two sides and less than the sum of those lengths.*\n\nLet's write that down using mathematical notations. Suppose that in a triangle the length of a side equals $a$ and the other two sides have length $b$ and $c$ with $b>c.$ Then, we must have \n\n$$\nb - c < a < b + c.\n$$\n\nIf we would like to omit the assumption that $b>c$, we can still use the same inequality, as long as we include an absolute value on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n| b - c |  < a < b + c\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084511 #edb/ref 1065 8194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084511 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cd8d2616-ebae-5d43-89d3-470c89118c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084511 #edb/ref 1067 "The Triangle Inequality Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve, *without using graphs*, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=1 \\\\ 5x-y &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we must combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation. One way to combine the equations is to use the **elimination method.** \n\nNotice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $1,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-1.$ Since these numbers add to zero, we can *add* the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{y} & =1 \\\\ 5x-\\cancel{y} & =11 \\\\ (x+5x) & =(1+11) \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (2)+y & =1 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2$ and $y=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(2, -1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1065 8702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "48f030ba-6728-565a-be53-cfb1e88abc86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose that we want to solve the following system of linear equations using elimination:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 \\\\ x-y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables. \n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/85/85996afab1889f8032a295f4db6bbbcbc8670a0bc12f0b64f53a3b809f111628.svg)\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This eliminates the variable $x,$ making it possible to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{x}+2y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{x}+y & =2 \\\\ (2y+y) & =(1+2) \\\\ 3y & =3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-y & =-2 \\\\ x-(1) & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1, 1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1065 8704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "170be181-ae77-50d7-a5f1-0a4bdcfa318e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1067 "Eliminating Variables From Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084746 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084746 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084746 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084746 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "03d1386f-01a8-5fc7-a5f6-2fb52b986e1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085124 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085124 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085124 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085124 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186525416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085124 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ca145851-e9d5-53d0-b524-37f7de7eeb0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085125 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085125 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085125 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186525515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085125 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6f29fa38-38be-5d7a-8b04-017c959f4e64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086454 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086454 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086454 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186525635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086454 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8bedc031-1af4-59be-8827-ddccc40055ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Equations Written in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8e2458e5-8061-5f3f-9b4f-4d0bdd0ec641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting from Slope-Intercept Form to Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f399f6ed-91bc-594e-9a40-b7c79f60c5dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting an Arbitrary Line to Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "daa37544-c4ec-5b8b-91a5-ccf5ee8f50d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Standard Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eefba93e-12c7-5835-80fb-b36463f140d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1045 "Equations of Lines in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cfe67386-85ee-52bc-a19f-9e5f91df386d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088002 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088002 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088002 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $-10+7y+5x=0$ in standard form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa966eb0-9844-5c98-a111-46249d704582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088002 #edb/ref 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n5x+7y=10.\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $5x+7y=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1061 178720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f6a809a-5a39-577f-8881-246e8ed35867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1061 178721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1825121-20e1-5854-9a35-360f43ffb277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1061 178725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ad80f23-5479-5329-8fd1-39cf7d32c8f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1061 178726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "219a19a2-5ca7-55ec-b664-706e0003f956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1061 178730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54390c19-603c-54bd-bf54-163d31414029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088548 #edb/ref 1061 178733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088548 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5756f274-95fd-5077-9cb0-2b24aadadb72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088548 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1064 "The **standard form** equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a \\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors.\n\nNote that we always write $x$ *before* $y$ on the left-hand side!\n\nLet's see some examples:\n\n- The equation $2x - y= -5$ is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2x - y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{-5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2,$ $-1,$ and $-5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($2$).\n\n- The equation $-3x + y = 1$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-3$).\n\n- The equation $2x+4y = -10$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,4,$ and $-10)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The equation $\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac13y = 6$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are not integers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1065 9126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "739ff387-5940-547f-adac-8b2ac8e5a229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now discuss how to convert the equation of a line to standard form.\n\nTo illustrate, we will take the line $y = 2x-1$ and convert it into standard form. We begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\ y-2x & =-1 \\\\ -2x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1065 9221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "914fb469-510e-5e74-92bc-6174870cbd47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1067 "Converting a Line to Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating Expressions With Zero Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129308 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "72c96e3f-e296-52c6-9c78-1b926625fe29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129309 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129309 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129